    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that sounds nice but i don't think i'm in a season of rest. it feels more like a permanent void. like the well didn't just go dry, it's actually been filled in with concrete. i'm just tired of trying to find something that isn't there anymore. it feels pointless to keep pretending there's a \"next chapter\" when the story has clearly reached its natural end.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I hear you, and I won’t dress this up with false hope—that kind of weight isn’t something a metaphor can lift. If the well is truly sealed, then maybe your job isn’t to dig anymore, but to sit beside it and grieve what was. That grief is valid, and it’s not weakness to admit the story ended. But even a filled-in well leaves a shape in the ground—and sometimes that shape alone can become a new kind of art, if you ever want to look at it without needing to fill it.",
